Name: stackAppearance

Type: property

Syntax: set the stackAppearance of <stack> to {empty | "light" | "dark" | "system"}

Syntax: get the [effective] stackAppearance of <stack>

Summary:
Specifies whether a <stack> is drawn in the light or the dark appearance,
or follows its mainstack or the <appAppearance>.

Associations: stack

Introduced: 9.7.1

OS: mac, windows, linux

Platforms: desktop

Example:
-- A palette that is always dark, whatever the appAppearance is
set the stackAppearance of stack "Tools" to "dark"

Example:
set the stackAppearance of this stack to empty -- inherit again

Example:
if the effective stackAppearance of this stack is "dark" then
   set the foreColor of field "Status" to "220,220,220"
end if

Parameters:
stack:
A reference to a <stack>.

Value:
The <stackAppearance> is empty, "light", "dark" or "system". Setting it
is not case-sensitive; getting it returns lower case. It is empty by
default: a substack then uses the <stackAppearance> of its mainstack, and
a mainstack uses the <appAppearance>. The menus the engine builds for a
button use the appearance of the button's stack. Setting it to any other
value, or setting it for an object that is not a stack, is an error.

The <effective> <stackAppearance> is "light" or "dark": the appearance
the stack's objects start from, after inheritance, with "system" resolved
to the operating system's setting (the <systemAppearance>).

Description:
Use the <stackAppearance> to draw one stack in another appearance than
the rest of the application. The IDE uses it to draw its own windows dark
while the stacks you are working on stay as they will be in your
standalone.

The <stackAppearance> is a runtime setting in this version: it is never
saved with the stack (a stack saved after setting it is the same file as
one saved without), it is not in <the properties>, it is empty when the
stack is loaded, and a <clone> of the stack gets the same value.

In the dark appearance every color a stack leaves unset comes from the
dark theme. Where the author set some colors and not others, the engine
fits the unset colors of each object to the explicit colors around it:
its own fill, its own text color on a default fill, and the panel, image,
group or card it sits on. A field with a white background and no text
color gets black text, a checkbox on a light card gets a black label and
a light box, and a button on a light panel gets a light native face. So a
stack designed light looks as designed in the dark appearance, and a
stack that sets no colors at all is drawn dark. When the engine cannot
tell, it uses the light appearance, because that is how existing stacks
were designed. Colors that were set are always drawn as set, and in the
light appearance every object is drawn exactly as before.

One case cannot be told apart: a transparent label or checkbox with a
dark text color of its own, on a card with no color, keeps its dark text
on the dark card. Give the card a color, or set the <stackAppearance> of
the stack to "light".

On Linux the native controls are drawn by the GTK theme, which the engine
cannot change: the appearance of every stack is the GTK theme's, whatever
the <stackAppearance> is set to. The setting is still stored and
returned.

On macOS every stack is drawn light in this version, whatever the
<stackAppearance> is set to (see <appAppearance>). The setting is still
stored and returned.

Setting the <stackAppearance> redraws the stack, its substacks and their
menus at once. It does not send <systemAppearanceChanged>.

Changes:
The <stackAppearance> was added in OXT-Beyond after release 0.1.0 (engine
9.7.1-OXT).

References: appAppearance (property), systemAppearance (property),
systemAppearanceChanged (message), effective (keyword), clone (command),
properties (property), stack (object)

Tags: ui
